<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e8f0f4; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Use of Technology in School – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Use of Technology in School</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Summarize the main points of different types of texts. Reading: Identify the main topic and related ideas in a simple structured text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can summarize and compare key points in two texts about technology in school (e.g., "One article says that using tablets helps students because they can find information quickly, while the other says it's distracting because students use them to play games."). Can identify the main topic and supporting ideas in a text about educational apps (e.g., "The main idea is that some apps can help students memorize information faster."). Can summarize articles on classroom technology use preferences (e.g., "Two reasons students dislike technology in the classroom include the lack of charging stations and poor internet connections.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various images of technology being used in classrooms (e.g., tablets, smartboards, laptops, educational apps). Students identify the devices and suggest how they are used. The teacher asks, "What kind of technology do you use in school?" and "Is it useful?" to activate prior knowledge and vocabulary (<i>technology, apps, device, use, useful, school</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes "I like technology in school" on the board and models the pronunciation, focusing on word stress in "technology" and "education". The teacher then models how to compare using "but" (e.g., "Tablets are useful, but sometimes they are distracting."). The teacher highlights the intonation patterns when expressing opinions and using modal verbs. For example, "You <b>should</b> focus" vs "I <b>can</b> use this".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you prefer using books or tablets for reading?" (To check understanding of 'prefer' and relate to technology use).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a device helps you learn faster, is it a <i>benefit</i> or a <i>problem</i>?" (To check understanding of key vocabulary).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short articles/blog posts (approx. 100-120 words each) titled "The Bright Side of Tech in Class" and "The Challenges of Digital Learning." The texts discuss the <i>benefits</i> (access to <i>information</i>, interactive <i>tools</i>) and <i>problems</i> (<i>distraction</i>, lack of <i>access</i>, poor <i>network</i>) of <i>technology</i> in <i>school</i>. They include vocabulary like <i>apps, device, innovation, benefit, problem, solution, access, platform, resources, use, ignore, prefer, engage</i>, and grammar points (simple present, "should" for suggestions, "can" for ability).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend information from different written sources on topics related to their interests, identifying main ideas and supporting details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read both articles quickly to find the main topic of each. "Which article talks about advantages? Which one talks about difficulties?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read statements about the articles and mark them as True or False. E.g., "Technology helps students find information quickly. (True/False)" "Students should always use their devices during lunch. (True/False)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Matching:</b> Match key vocabulary from the texts (e.g., <i>benefit, problem, solution, access, network</i>) with their definitions or synonyms.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying main topics and supporting ideas, summarizing, and using simple present for opinions/preferences, modals "should" and "can", Wh-questions, and target vocabulary (<i>technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, device, access, use, prefer, advise, limit</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-Fill Summary:</b> Students complete a cloze text that summarizes one of the articles, using a word bank with target vocabulary (e.g., "Article 1 talks about the ______ of using technology. For example, students can ______ information easily. However, one ______ is that it can be distracting."). Focus on <i>benefit, problem, access, use</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Transformation (Modals):</b> Students rephrase sentences from the articles or general statements about technology using "should" or "can". E.g., "Teachers advise students to use apps for studying." (Students should use apps for studying.) "Students learn faster with interactive tools." (Students can learn faster with interactive tools.) The teacher will model stress in "should" and "can" and minimal pairs (/ʊ/ in "should" and /uː/ in "shooed") if relevant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Identify Main Idea & Details:</b> In pairs, students re-read paragraphs from both texts and highlight the main topic sentence in one color and two supporting details in another. They then compare their highlighted sentences with their partner, discussing "What is the main idea here?" using Wh-questions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Summary and Comparison. Students work in pairs to summarize and compare the main points of the two articles they read. They will use a graphic organizer (e.g., a Venn diagram or a T-chart) to organize their thoughts before writing a short comparative summary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher distributes a T-chart with two columns: "Benefits of Technology" (from Article 1) and "Problems with Technology" (from Article 2).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. In pairs, students fill in the chart with key points identified from their reading. They use simple present tense to state points (e.g., "Technology offers quick access to information.") and relevant vocabulary (<i>benefit, problem, access, resources, distraction, network</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Using their filled charts, each pair writes a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) summarizing the main points of both texts and comparing them. They are encouraged to use discourse markers like <i>while, but, instead, because</i>. E.g., "One article says that using tablets helps students because they can find information quickly, while the other says it's distracting because students use them to play games." They should also integrate "should" and "can" where appropriate (e.g., "We should limit screen time.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Pairs exchange their summaries with another pair for peer-checking for clarity and logical flow, focusing on identifying main ideas and using linking words.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to summarize, identify main ideas, and compare texts, as well as their use of target grammar and vocabulary. Peer assessment of written summaries using a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each pair submits their written comparative summary. The teacher collects and reviews them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly summarizes main points from both texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectively compares and contrasts ideas from both texts using discourse markers (<i>while, but</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of simple present tense for opinions and factual statements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal verbs "should" and "can" in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporates target vocabulary (<i>technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, access, distraction</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall clarity and coherence of the written summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one main benefit of technology in school mentioned in the texts?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one main problem of technology in school mentioned in the texts?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about reading today is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about summarizing the two texts and comparing them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can identifying the main idea help me understand texts better in the future?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the lesson? (e.g., specific activities, student engagement, clarity of instructions). What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to identify main ideas or synthesize information? How effectively did students use the target grammar and vocabulary in their summaries? Which pronunciation points (e.g., stress in "education," intonation with "should") require more practice?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the summarized information and their opinions from today's reading to engage in discussions and debates about the appropriate use of technology in school, offering suggestions and weighing benefits and drawbacks more actively in a spoken context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>